research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/cex-dense-set-of-measure-zero.md-220-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/cex-dense-set-of-measure-zero.md-221-- **The cover is explicit and startling.** For every $\varepsilon > 0$ the rationals are covered by open intervals of total length exactly $\varepsilon$ ([[ex-q-covered-by-intervals-of-small-total-length]]), although their union is dense because it contains $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$.
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/cex-dense-set-of-measure-zero.md-222-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/cex-dense-set-of-measure-zero.md:223:- **$\mathbb{Q}$ is small in the other sense too, one level up.** It is meager, being a countable union of singletons ([[cor-q-is-meager-and-not-g-delta]]); what fails is only nowhere density itself. So the counterexample separates "nowhere dense" from "meager", not category from measure.
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/cex-dense-set-of-measure-zero.md-224-
research/audit/wave8-certifier-tasks/cex-dense-set-of-measure-zero.md-225-- **The complementary witness** is the Smith-Volterra-Cantor set, nowhere dense and not null ([[cex-nowhere-dense-with-positive-measure]]).
