research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-594-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-595-- **The classes are genuinely larger than the closed and the open sets.**
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-596-  $\mathbb{Q}$ is $F_\sigma$ and is neither open nor closed, and the irrationals
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-597-  are $G_\delta$ and neither open nor closed; both computations are in
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md:598:  [[cor-q-is-meager-and-not-g-delta]]. That $\mathbb{Q}$ is not also $G_\delta$
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-599-  is the first genuinely hard fact about these classes and needs the Baire
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-600-  category theorem ([[thm-baire-category-r]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-601-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-602-- **Why the algebra of open sets is not enough.** [[thm-open-set-algebra-r]]
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-603-  gives that a *finite* intersection of open sets is open and a *finite* union of
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-604-  closed sets is closed. The definitions above are exactly what one gets by
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-605-  relaxing "finite" to "countable" once, and the point of the whole notion is
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/fs-q-is-g-delta.md-606-  that the relaxation is proper: a countable intersection of open sets need not
