research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-irrationals-are-not-f-sigma.md-693-[A1] The false claim: $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ is a $G_\delta$ subset of $\mathbb{R}$.
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-irrationals-are-not-f-sigma.md-694-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-irrationals-are-not-f-sigma.md:695:[L1] $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ is $F_\sigma$ and meager, the irrationals are $G_\delta$ and residual, and $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ is **not** $G_\delta$ ([[cor-q-is-meager-and-not-g-delta]], claims 1, 2 and 3).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-irrationals-are-not-f-sigma.md-696-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-irrationals-are-not-f-sigma.md-697-[L2] $A$ is $G_\delta$ when it is the intersection of a sequence of open sets ([[def-f-sigma-g-delta]], [[def-open-and-closed-in-r]]).
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-irrationals-are-not-f-sigma.md-698-
research/audit/wave8-refuter-tasks/cex-irrationals-are-not-f-sigma.md-699-[L3] A countable intersection of dense open subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ is dense; in particular it is nonempty ([[thm-baire-category-r]]).
