research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-290830-+{"id":"def-measure-zero-and-content-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf","https://www.cs.uaf.edu/~maxwell/AY2007/math641/Measure.pdf"],"rationale":"MIT uses the epsilon-small countable interval-cover definition of nullity, while the UAF notes give the countable-cover formulation and the compact finite-subcover reduction. The local term content zero is a deliberately elementary finite-cover convention, so the package is a semantic restatement rather than an exact quotation of Jordan measure.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-290831-+{"id":"lem-finite-interval-cover-total-length","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.cs.uaf.edu/~maxwell/AY2007/math641/Measure.pdf"],"rationale":"The UAF notes state the interval-cover length estimate used in the measure-zero argument and reduce compact interval covers to finite subcovers. The local proof changes the finite-overlap organization but proves the same covering estimate.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-290832-+{"id":"lem-nondegenerate-interval-is-not-null","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.cs.uaf.edu/~maxwell/AY2007/math641/Measure.pdf"],"rationale":"The UAF notes give the countable interval-cover argument showing that an interval of positive length is not null, using compactness to pass to a finite subcover. The local proof follows that argument with this library’s series indexing.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-290833- {"id":"lem-countable-sets-are-null","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf"],"rationale":"MIT Homework 2.1 states exactly that every countable subset of Euclidean space has measure zero. The local proof is the standard geometric-budget cover, adapted to zero-based indexing and the library’s countability convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-290834- {"id":"thm-countable-union-of-null-is-null","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf"],"rationale":"MIT Homework 2.1 states exactly that a countable union of measure-zero sets has measure zero. The local proof expands the standard double-indexed epsilon allocation and explicitly records countable choice.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-290835- {"id":"lem-content-zero-implies-null","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.125/HW2.pdf"],"rationale":"Finite covers are a special case of the countable interval covers in the MIT definition. The implication is a direct local verification from the two definitions; its proof is repository-local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
